 &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;We present the Seismic Attribute Selection Guide, a structured, browser-based instrument that helps geoscientists select a robust, defensible starting set of seismic attributes for multi-attribute interpretation and machine learning (ML) workflows. The tool addresses a persistent problem in seismic interpretation practice: attributes are routinely computed without a geologic rationale, in sets that are either too small to capture the relevant physical properties of the target or too large to be meaningfully different from one another, and without adequate attention to the preprocessing conditions that determine whether a given attribute will measure geology or noise.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The tool asks sixteen questions across three sections – 1) data conditioning and preprocessing, 2) geologic target, and 3) workflow goals - and produces three outputs: a color-coded preprocessing checklist identifying steps that must be completed before attribute extraction, and a two-tiered attribute recommendation (core set and consider adding) with plain-language explanations linking each attribute to the specific physical properties of the user’s target. Attribute recommendations are calibrated to fourteen primary geologic targets spanning stratigraphy, structure, fluid indicators, chaotic geobodies, and near-surface hazards.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The instrument places attributes developed and implemented by the AASPI software suite - including the Distance Quadrant (DQ) trace and Theta PX for AVO characterization, aberrancy for small-offset sub-seismic fault detection, nonparallelism for chaotic facies discrimination, reflector convergence for channel characterization, thin bed decomposition with phase components, and the full suite of spectral decomposition programs - in the primary recommendation tier rather than as supplementary additions. Every attribute card identifies the exact AASPI program name, output file naming convention, and the physical rationale connecting the attribute to the target. The tool is software-independent; AASPI attributes are flagged clearly so non-AASPI users can identify capabilities they may be missing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This paper describes the rationale, design principles, recommendation logic, and intended use of the tool. We document the attribute computation failure modes the instrument is designed to prevent, situate the tool within the existing literature on seismic attribute analysis and multi-attribute ML workflows, and discuss its applications in industry practice, research, and geoscience education.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
